# Batch 27 (2026-11-14): Patagonia, Tierra del Fuego and the Southern Cone (myths, giants, ghost ships, lost cities);
# the Caucasus and the Yoruba world; origins of death, moon myths and the first farmers; witch trials and two late-Soviet/British UFO cases
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: Patagonia, Tierra del Fuego and the Southern Cone
E+=[
X('chinchorro-mummies','The Chinchorro mummies: the world\'s oldest deliberately preserved dead',H,'confirmed',2,-5050,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "On the desert coast of northern Chile and southern Peru, the Chinchorro, a fishing and gathering people, began artificially mummifying their dead by about 5050 BCE, some two thousand years before the earliest known deliberate mummification in Egypt. The oldest naturally preserved Chinchorro body dates to about 7020 BCE. Unlike in Egypt, the treatment was given to men, women, children and even foetuses rather than to an elite. Bodies were taken apart, the flesh removed, the skeleton reinforced with sticks and reeds, then rebuilt with clay, ash and plant fibre, covered with the person's own skin, given wigs of human hair and painted black with manganese or red with ochre. The tradition lasted about 3,000 years, ending around 1800 BCE. Chinchorro sites in Chile's Arica and Parinacota region became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2021. Conservators report that some mummies in museum collections have begun to degrade as the humid air of the region increases.",
 [('Why mummify everyone?','One proposal, by the anthropologist Bernardo Arriaza and colleagues, links the custom to high levels of arsenic in local drinking water, which may have caused many stillbirths and infant deaths; preserving the dead might have been a response to grief. This is a hypothesis, not proven.'),
  ('A link to Egypt?','Because the Chinchorro predate Egyptian mummification, some popular writers have wondered whether the practice spread from one to the other. Archaeologists see the two as independent inventions on opposite sides of the world, with no evidence of contact.')],
 ['wiki:Chinchorro_mummies','wiki:Chinchorro_culture'],img='Chinchorro mummies',end=-1800),
X('ten-ten-vilu','Ten Ten-Vilu and Kai Kai-Vilu: the Mapuche serpents of land and sea who fought over a flood',M,'myth-legend',2,1600,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "In Mapuche tradition of southern Chile and the island of Chiloé, two great serpents embody the opposing forces of the world. Kai Kai-Vilu (also Caicai or Kai Kai Filu), the serpent of the sea, raised the waters to drown humanity; Ten Ten-Vilu (Trentren), the serpent of the earth, raised the hills and mountains so that people could climb to safety. Their battle, in which some people were turned into fish, sea lions or rocks, is said to explain the broken coastline and islands of the Chiloé archipelago. Mapuche communities have sacred hills called trentren where they can take refuge. The story was recorded by Spanish chroniclers from the colonial period onwards, and versions are still told today.",
 [('Memory of real tsunamis','The Chilean coast is one of the most earthquake- and tsunami-prone in the world (the 1960 Valdivia earthquake is the largest ever recorded). Many researchers see the myth as a traditional explanation of, and guide to surviving, tsunamis: when the sea rises, go to the high ground. Whether it preserves memory of particular events is unknown.'),
  ('One of many world flood myths','Comparative mythologists note the resemblance to flood myths elsewhere in which survivors escape to a mountain; some see a common human pattern, others a widely shared response to local floods. Diffusion from Christian missionaries has also been suggested for some details.')],
 ['wiki:Ten_Ten-Vilu','wiki:Mapuche_religion'],img='File:Cai Cai Ancud.png',dateNote='Oral tradition; recorded from the colonial period onwards'),
X('selknam-hain','The Hain: the Selk\'nam ceremony where men played the spirits, and the myth that women once ruled',H,'confirmed',2,1923,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "The Selk'nam (Ona) of Tierra del Fuego held a long male initiation ceremony called the Hain, which could last for months when food was plentiful (for example after a whale stranding). Young men were taken into a large hut, where they were confronted by 'spirits', men in body paint and masks, until they learned that the spirits were their own relatives. They were then told the founding myth: in ancient times women, led by the Moon (Kreeh), ruled through a secret lodge where they disguised themselves as spirits, until men discovered the trick, killed the women and took over the ceremony. Painted spirits such as Shoort then visited the women's camp in dramatic performances. The last full Hain was held in 1923 and photographed by the missionary and ethnologist Martin Gusinde, whose images are now famous. Within a few decades the Selk'nam population had collapsed through settler violence, disease and forced resettlement, now often described as the Selk'nam genocide.",
 [('A myth of matriarchy?','Some writers have read the myth as memory of an ancient period of female rule. Most anthropologists see it as a story that justified male control of ritual rather than as history; similar \'women once owned the sacred things\' myths are found among peoples in Amazonia and Melanesia.'),
  ('Did the women know?','Gusinde\'s informants said the women were deceived; other observers, and later scholars, have suggested the women knew the spirits were men and took part in a shared performance.')],
 ['wiki:Selkʼnam_people','wiki:Selkʼnam_genocide'],img='File:El Hain A74352820250105.jpg',dateNote='Ceremony of long but unknown antiquity; last full Hain 1923'),
X('caleuche','The Caleuche: the glowing ghost ship of Chiloé',M,'myth-legend',2,1700,['oral tradition'],
 "In the folklore of the Chiloé archipelago in southern Chile, the Caleuche is a ship that sails at night through the channels, brightly lit and full of music and the noise of a party. It is crewed by brujos (sorcerers) and the souls of the drowned, can travel underwater, and can turn itself into a rock or a floating log, while its sailors can turn into seals or sea birds. It is said to kidnap people and to make rich the merchants who trade with it secretly, a story sometimes used to explain sudden wealth in a community. The name has been derived from the Mapudungun word kalewche, 'transformed people'. It is linked to the wider Chilote belief in a society of sorcerers, the Recta Provincia, which was the subject of a real trial in 1880–81 in Ancud.",
 [('Smugglers and lights','Rationalising explanations suggest the legend was reinforced by smugglers working at night, or by bioluminescence and mirages in the fjords. These ideas are speculative.'),
  ('European ghost-ship lore','Scholars note parallels with European legends such as the Flying Dutchman, brought by sailors, mixed with Indigenous ideas of transformation and the sea.')],
 ['wiki:Caleuche','wiki:Chilote_mythology'],img='File:Caleuche A74067820240105.jpg',dateNote='Folklore recorded from the 19th century; older origins unknown'),
X('city-of-the-caesars','The City of the Caesars: the enchanted lost city of Patagonia',M,'myth-legend',2,1528,['textual'],
 "For more than two centuries Spanish colonists searched the southern Andes for the Ciudad de los Césares, a rich city said to be hidden in a Patagonian valley, built of gold and silver and sometimes said to be visible only at certain times, or forgotten by anyone who stumbled on it. Its supposed founders changed with the teller: survivors of the 1528 expedition of Francisco César (from whom the name may come), Spanish castaways from shipwrecks in the Strait of Magellan, refugees from the destroyed Spanish towns of southern Chile after 1598, or Inca nobles fleeing the conquest. Expeditions set out from Chile and Argentina into the 18th century; in 1766 the Jesuit José García Alsue searched the area now in Queulat National Park. No city was ever found.",
 [('Echo of the Inca Empire','One explanation is that the legend grew out of Indigenous reports of the wealthy Inca Empire to the north, carried south and misunderstood. Others connect it to real castaway communities.'),
  ('Still out there','A few modern seekers and novelists keep the idea of a hidden valley alive; there is no archaeological evidence for such a settlement.')],
 ['wiki:City_of_the_Caesars','wiki:El_Dorado'],img='File:Patagonia in Gutiérrez, the Americas, 1562 (cropped).jpg',end=1766),
X('cueva-de-las-manos','Cueva de las Manos: nine thousand years of hands on a Patagonian cave wall',H,'confirmed',2,-7300,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "In the Pinturas River canyon in Santa Cruz province, Argentina, the Cueva de las Manos ('Cave of the Hands') holds more than 2,000 hand images, most of them stencilled left hands (829 left to 31 right in one count), made by blowing pigment through bone tubes. Alongside them are paintings of guanacos, hunting scenes, rheas, felines and geometric shapes. The art was made in several phases from about 7300 BCE to about 700 CE by hunter-gatherer peoples, possibly ancestors of the Tehuelche. Dating comes from bone pipes found in the deposits, radiocarbon dates and the layering of images. It was listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1999.",
 [('Missing fingers and messages','Some hands appear to lack fingers. This may reflect injury or deformity, but some researchers suggest fingers were deliberately bent to make signs, perhaps a gesture language; this cannot be confirmed.'),
  ('A worldwide gesture','Hand stencils are found from Sulawesi and Borneo to Spain and Australia, some made more than 40,000 years ago. Some see a deep shared human impulse; others stress that the meanings almost certainly differed from place to place.')],
 ['wiki:Cueva_de_las_Manos','wiki:Cave_painting'],img='Cueva de las Manos',end=700),
X('jemmy-button','Jemmy Button: the Fuegian boy bought for a button and taken to meet the King',H,'confirmed',2,1830,['textual'],
 "In 1830 Captain Robert FitzRoy of HMS Beagle took four young Fuegians to England after a whaleboat was stolen. One, a Yaghan (Yámana) boy named Orundellico, was renamed Jemmy Button because FitzRoy wrote that he had been bought for a mother-of-pearl button. In England the Fuegians (one of whom, Boat Memory, died of smallpox) were educated, became celebrities and met King William IV and Queen Adelaide. FitzRoy took the three survivors home on the second voyage of the Beagle in 1831–33, with the young Charles Darwin aboard. Jemmy soon returned to Yaghan life and declined to go back to England. In 1859 missionaries were killed at Wulaia Bay, and Jemmy was accused of involvement but denied it at an inquiry in the Falklands. He died around 1864.",
 [('How was he taken?','FitzRoy\'s account of buying him for a button is the only source for the name; some historians suspect it softened what was really a kidnapping.'),
  ('Darwin\'s view','Darwin\'s encounter with the Fuegians shaped his views of human nature; his descriptions have been criticised as prejudiced, while he also noted how quickly the Fuegians in England had become like the English, which he used to argue for the unity of humankind.')],
 ['wiki:Jemmy_Button','wiki:Second_voyage_of_HMS_Beagle'],img='Jemmy Button',end=1864),
X('valdivia-1960','The 1960 Valdivia earthquake: the strongest ever recorded, and a sacrifice to calm the sea',H,'confirmed',2,1960,['textual','geological/ice core'],
 "On 22 May 1960 a megathrust earthquake of magnitude about 9.4–9.6, the strongest ever measured, struck southern Chile and shook for around ten minutes. Tsunami waves up to about 25 metres hit the Chilean coast, and the main tsunami crossed the Pacific, killing 61 people in Hilo, Hawaii, and around 140 in Japan. Estimates of the total death toll range from about 1,000 to 6,000. Two days later the Cordón Caulle volcano began erupting. In the isolated Mapuche (Lafkenche) community of Collileufu near Lake Budi, while the people sheltered on a hill from the repeated tsunamis, a local machi (shaman) called for the sacrifice of a five-year-old boy, José Luis Painecur, to calm the earth and the sea; he was killed. Two men confessed and were charged, later recanted, and were released after two years; a judge ruled they had acted 'without free will, driven by an irresistible natural force of ancestral tradition'.",
 [('Ritual or crime?','The case is cited by anthropologists as evidence that the old Mapuche idea of offering to calm Kai Kai-Vilu and the sea survived into the 20th century; others caution that it was an isolated act by individuals under extreme stress, and should not be used to stereotype Mapuche religion.'),
  ('Earthquakes and tsunamis in myth','The event is often linked to the Ten Ten-Vilu and Kai Kai-Vilu story, which tells people to climb high ground when the sea rises, the same advice modern tsunami warnings give.')],
 ['wiki:1960_Valdivia_earthquake','wiki:Puyehue-Cordón_Caulle'],img='1960 Valdivia earthquake'),
X('luison','The Luisón: the seventh son who becomes a beast, and the Argentine president as godfather',M,'myth-legend',3,1907,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "In Guaraní mythology of Paraguay and north-eastern Argentina, the Luisón (or lobizón) is the seventh and last son of the evil spirit Tau and the woman Kerana, one of seven monstrous brothers; in later folk belief, any seventh son in a family of boys was at risk of turning into a dog- or wolf-like creature that haunts cemeteries. Folklorists link the wolf form to European werewolf beliefs brought by settlers, perhaps inspired by the long-legged maned wolf. In Argentina a custom grew up of the President becoming godparent to seventh sons, said to protect them from abandonment; the first official presidential godparent baptism was recorded in 1907, and in the 1970s, under Juan Perón, it was given legal form, with scholarships for the child. The custom continues, and there have been proposals to abolish it as based on superstition.",
 [('Seventh-son lore worldwide','In European folklore a seventh son (or seventh son of a seventh son) is often said to have healing powers or second sight; in the Río de la Plata region the belief took a darker turn. Folklorists see the Luisón as a blend of Guaraní and European traditions.'),
  ('Superstition or welfare?','Popular accounts say the presidential custom was meant to stop families abandoning or harming seventh sons out of fear; today supporters describe it mainly as help for large families, while critics call it an outdated relic of superstition.')],
 ['wiki:Luison','wiki:Guarani_mythology'],img='Luisón'),
X('mylodon-cave','The Mylodon Cave: the giant sloth skin that seemed fresh enough to be alive',H,'confirmed',3,1895,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "In 1895–96 settlers near Puerto Natales in Chilean Patagonia found, in a large cave on Cerro Benítez, a piece of skin with hair and bony nodules, along with bones and dung, so well preserved that some thought the animal might still be alive. It belonged to Mylodon, a giant ground sloth about the size of an ox, named from remains Charles Darwin had collected during the Beagle voyage. The finds launched expeditions, including one in 1900 by the journalist Hesketh Hesketh-Prichard, to search Patagonia for a living giant sloth; none was found. Radiocarbon dating shows the remains are around 10,000–13,000 years old, preserved by the cold, dry conditions. The cave also holds evidence of early human presence, and became famous through Bruce Chatwin's book In Patagonia (1977), inspired by a piece of the skin owned by his grandmother's cousin.",
 [('A surviving sloth?','Reports of the Patagonian \'iemisch\' and the Amazonian mapinguari have kept alive the idea of a surviving ground sloth. Zoologists consider this very unlikely given the absence of any recent remains.'),
  ('Kept by humans?','Early researchers suggested people had penned the sloths in the cave like livestock; this was later rejected, and the remains are thought to reflect natural deaths and some human hunting.')],
 ['wiki:Cueva_del_Milodón_Natural_Monument','wiki:Mylodon'],img='Cueva del Milodón Natural Monument',dateNote='Remains c. 10,000–13,000 years old; found 1895–96'),
X('patagonian-giants','The Patagonian giants: three centuries of European tales of a land of giants',H,'debunked',2,1520,['textual'],
 "In 1520, during Ferdinand Magellan's voyage around the world, the chronicler Antonio Pigafetta described meeting on the Patagonian coast a man so tall that 'we reached only to his waist'. Magellan called the people 'Patagones', probably after a giant in the Spanish chivalric romance Primaleón (1512), not 'big feet' as was long assumed, and the region was labelled regio gigantum on maps. Later voyagers repeated the claim: Francis Drake's chaplain wrote of men 'seven foot and a half', and in 1766 rumours spread that John Byron's crew on HMS Dolphin had seen nine-foot natives. When an official account appeared in 1773 the figure was 6 feet 6 inches. The people in question were probably Tehuelche (Aónikenk), who were indeed notably tall by European standards of the time.",
 [('Exaggeration and expectation','Historians see the legend as a mix of real height differences, the bulky guanaco-skin cloaks the Tehuelche wore, travellers\' exaggeration and expectations shaped by romance and classical tales of giants at the edge of the world.'),
  ('Bones of giants','In 1615 Schouten and Le Maire reported graves with giant bones at Puerto Deseado; these may have been fossils of large extinct animals, an example of the wider pattern of fossils being read as giants.')],
 ['wiki:Patagons','wiki:Tehuelche_people'],img='Patagonian giants',end=1773),
]
# --- part 2: the Caucasus and the Yoruba world; thunderstones
E+=[
X('nart-sagas','The Nart sagas: the Caucasus epics of Sosruko, Satanaya and the sword thrown into the sea',M,'myth-legend',2,-500,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "The Nart sagas are a body of heroic tales shared by peoples of the North Caucasus, including the Ossetians, Circassians (Adyghe and Kabardians), Abkhaz, Abazins, Karachay-Balkars and, in part, Chechens and Ingush. They tell of the Narts, a race of heroes: Sosruko (Soslan), born from a stone and tempered by the smith-god Tlepsh, invulnerable except at his knees; Satanaya, the wise matriarch; the trickster Syrdon; and Batraz, who when dying ordered that his sword be thrown into the sea, and knew when his companions lied about having done it. The tales were first written down in the 19th century but scholars agree that the Ossetian versions preserve an ancient Iranian core inherited from the Scythians, Sarmatians and Alans, mixed with even older North Caucasian material. The French scholar Georges Dumézil used them as a key source for his theory of three social functions (priests, warriors, producers) in Indo-European myth.",
 [('From Scythia to Camelot','C. Scott Littleton and Linda Malcor argued (1994) that the Arthurian legends drew on Sarmatian traditions brought by 5,500 Sarmatian cavalrymen the Romans posted to Britain in 175 CE, pointing to the parallel between Batraz\'s sword and Excalibur. Many Arthurian scholars think the parallels are suggestive but the transmission unproven.'),
  ('Shared Indo-European heritage','Others see the resemblances to Greek (Achilles\' heel), Norse (Loki) and Arthurian stories as common inheritance from much older Indo-European myth, or as independent invention.')],
 ['wiki:Nart_saga','wiki:Ossetian_mythology'],img='File:SAWSROUQA.jpg',dateNote='Oral tradition with roots in Scythian–Sarmatian times; written down from the 19th century'),
X('urartu','Urartu: the forgotten kingdom of Ararat that rivalled Assyria',H,'confirmed',2,-860,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Urartu was an Iron Age kingdom centred on Lake Van in the Armenian highlands, spanning parts of modern Turkey, Armenia, Iran and Iraq. Its kings, who called their land Biainili, wrote in cuneiform in the Urartian language, related to Hurrian, and built great fortresses, canals and reservoirs. Its capital was Tushpa (at Van), and its chief god was Haldi. In 782 BCE King Argishti I founded the fortress of Erebuni, whose name survives in Yerevan, the capital of Armenia. Urartu fought Assyria for two centuries; in 714 BCE Sargon II's army sacked the Haldi temple at Musasir, described in a famous letter to the god Ashur. The kingdom collapsed in the early 6th century BCE, and fortresses such as Teishebaini (Karmir Blur) were burned. The Assyrian name Urartu is the same word as the biblical 'Ararat'; the Bible's 'mountains of Ararat', where Noah's ark came to rest, and the 'land of Ararat' where the murderers of Sennacherib fled, refer to this kingdom.",
 [('Which mountain?','Genesis places the ark on \'the mountains of Ararat\', meaning the region of Urartu, not a single peak. The identification with the volcano now called Mount Ararat (Ağrı Dağı) came later; other traditions, such as the Syriac and Quranic, point to Mount Judi further south.'),
  ('Ancestors of the Armenians?','Historians debate how far the Urartians contributed to the formation of the Armenian people, who appear in the region soon after Urartu fell; Armenian national history often stresses continuity, while others emphasise the arrival of Armenian speakers.')],
 ['wiki:Urartu','wiki:Erebuni_Fortress'],img='Erebuni Fortress',end=-590),
X('vardzia','Vardzia: Queen Tamar\'s cave city of thirteen storeys',H,'confirmed',3,1185,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Vardzia is a monastery and town carved into the cliffs above the Kura River in southern Georgia. Begun under King Giorgi III (1156–84) and greatly expanded under his daughter, Queen Tamar, it once held hundreds of rooms on up to 19 tiers along about 500 metres of cliff, with churches, a throne room, wine cellars, tunnels and an irrigation system, able to shelter thousands of people from Turkish and Mongol raids. The Church of the Dormition, carved in the 1180s, contains rare wall paintings of Tamar and her father. In 1283 an earthquake sheared off much of the outer cliff, exposing the honeycomb of chambers that is visible today. The monastery was sacked by Persian forces in 1551 and later abandoned under Ottoman rule.",
 [('The legend of the name','A popular story says the young Tamar got lost in the caves while hunting and called out \'Ak var, dzia!\' (\'I\'m here, uncle!\'), giving the place its name. Historians regard this as folk etymology; the name appears to be older.'),
  ('A hidden refuge','Vardzia is often compared with other cave cities, such as Cappadocia\'s underground towns, as an example of whole communities going underground for safety; how many people lived there at its height is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Vardzia','wiki:Tamar_of_Georgia'],img='Vardzia',end=1551),
X('gates-of-alexander','The Gates of Alexander: the iron wall against Gog and Magog',M,'myth-legend',2,700,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Ancient and medieval writers told that Alexander the Great had built a wall of iron and bronze in a mountain pass to shut out barbarous northern peoples. The 1st-century historian Josephus linked the Scythians with Magog, and the 7th-century Syriac Alexander Legend had Alexander enclose the nations of Gog and Magog, who would break out at the end of time. The Quran (Sura 18) tells of Dhu al-Qarnayn, 'the two-horned one', who built a barrier of iron and molten copper against Ya'juj and Ma'juj. The gates were placed at real fortifications, especially the Sasanian Persian walls at Derbent on the Caspian and the Darial gorge in the Caucasus, and the Great Wall of Gorgan. In 842 the Caliph al-Wathiq, after dreaming the wall had been breached, sent an official, Sallam the Interpreter, to inspect it; Sallam returned more than two years later, reporting the wall intact. Medieval maps show Gog and Magog walled up in the far north-east.",
 [('Which wall?','Historians note the Caucasus walls were built by the Sasanians centuries after Alexander. Scholars debate whether Sallam reached Derbent, the Jade Gate of China, or another fortification.'),
  ('Dhu al-Qarnayn\'s identity','Many Muslim and Western scholars identify Dhu al-Qarnayn with Alexander, through the Syriac legend; some Muslim scholars prefer Cyrus the Great or a South Arabian king, and some modern writers have suggested the wall is yet to be found. Believers read the end-times prophecy as still to come.')],
 ['wiki:Gates_of_Alexander','wiki:Gog_and_Magog'],img='File:Iskandar (Alexander) builds a wall against Gog and Magog (CBL Per. 395.1).jpg',dateNote='Legend c. 1st–7th centuries; Sallam\'s journey 842'),
X('khazar-conversion','The Khazars: the steppe empire whose rulers became Jewish',H,'disputed',2,740,['textual','archaeological','genetic'],
 "The Khazars were a Turkic people who built a powerful multi-ethnic khaganate between the Volga, the Don and the North Caucasus from about 650 until the 960s, controlling trade between the Byzantine world, the Caliphate and the Rus. Arabic, Hebrew and other sources report that the khagan and ruling elite adopted Rabbinic Judaism, probably in the 8th or 9th century, while much of the population remained pagan, Christian or Muslim. A Hebrew letter attributed to King Joseph of Khazaria (part of the 'Khazar Correspondence' with Hasdai ibn Shaprut of Córdoba, 10th century) tells how an earlier king, Bulan, chose Judaism after hearing the case for each faith, a story the poet Judah Halevi retold in his philosophical dialogue the Kuzari (c. 1140). The khaganate was broken by Prince Sviatoslav of Kiev around 965–969. How widespread the conversion was, and the authenticity of parts of the Correspondence, are debated.",
 [('Ancestors of Ashkenazi Jews?','The idea that Ashkenazi Jews descend largely from Khazar converts was popularised by Arthur Koestler\'s The Thirteenth Tribe (1976) and argued genetically by Eran Elhaik (2012). Most genetic studies, such as Behar et al. (2013), find no substantial Khazar ancestry and point to Middle Eastern and European origins; most scholars regard the hypothesis as unsupported. It has also been used in antisemitic conspiracy theories, which historians and geneticists reject.'),
  ('How deep was the conversion?','Some historians, such as Moshe Gil and Shaul Stampfer, have argued the conversion is poorly evidenced or even legendary; others point to Khazar coins struck c. 837–838 with the legend \'Moses is the messenger of God\' as supporting evidence.')],
 ['wiki:Khazars','wiki:Khazar_Correspondence','doi:10.1093/gbe/evs119','doi:10.3378/027.085.0604'],img='Khazars',end=969),
X('oyo-empire','The Oyo Empire: cavalry, kingmakers and the king who was handed parrot\'s eggs',H,'confirmed',2,1600,['textual','oral tradition','archaeological'],
 "Oyo was the largest Yoruba state, centred on Oyo-Ile (Old Oyo) in the savanna of what is now western Nigeria. Tradition traces its founding to Oranmiyan, a prince of Ile-Ife. After being driven into exile by the Nupe in the 16th century, Oyo rebuilt itself with a powerful cavalry, and from the late 16th to the 18th century dominated most of Yorubaland and made the kingdom of Dahomey pay tribute. The king, the Alaafin, was checked by a council of nobles, the Oyo Mesi, led by the Bashorun, which could reject a king by presenting him with an empty calabash or parrot's eggs, a sign that he must take his own life. In the 18th century the Bashorun Gaha used this power to force several Alaafins to die before he was overthrown in 1774. Weakened by intrigue, the revolt of the general Afonja at Ilorin and the jihad of the Sokoto Caliphate, Oyo-Ile was abandoned in the 1830s, and a new Oyo was founded further south. The ruins of Oyo-Ile lie in Old Oyo National Park.",
 [('Constitutional monarchy?','Some historians describe Oyo\'s system of checks on the king as an early form of constitutional government; others caution against applying modern terms, noting the power the system gave to factions such as Gaha\'s.'),
  ('Oral tradition and dates','Much early Oyo history depends on king lists and praise poetry recorded in the 19th century, notably by Samuel Johnson in The History of the Yorubas; historians debate how literally to take its chronology.')],
 ['wiki:Oyo_Empire','wiki:Old_Oyo_National_Park'],img='Oyo Empire',end=1836),
X('shango','Shango: the king of Oyo who became the god of thunder',M,'myth-legend',2,1400,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "Ṣàngó (Shango) is the Yoruba orisha of thunder, lightning, fire, dance and justice, symbolised by a double-headed axe. In Oyo tradition he was also a historical king, the third Alaafin of Oyo, after Oranmiyan and Ajaka: a powerful and fiery ruler who could breathe fire and command lightning. In one version his magic brought lightning that destroyed his own palace, and in grief or shame he hanged himself at Koso; when his enemies mocked, his followers declared 'Oba kò so' ('the king did not hang') and calamities, including fires caused by lightning, were blamed on his angry spirit, which was then worshipped. Shango's cult was central to the Oyo state, and through the Atlantic slave trade he became Xangô in Brazilian Candomblé and Changó in Cuban Santería, often identified with Saint Barbara.",
 [('A god who was a man','The story is a classic case of what scholars call euhemerism, a human king becoming a god. Some historians think a real early Alaafin was merged with an older thunder deity, Jakuta or Ara; believers see Shango as a living spiritual force regardless of history.'),
  ('Thunder axes worldwide','Shango\'s shrines often hold prehistoric stone axe heads (edun ara, \'thunderstones\') believed to be thunderbolts he has hurled, a belief strikingly similar to European, Asian and Native American thunderstone traditions.')],
 ['wiki:Shango','wiki:Oyo_Empire','wiki:Yoruba_religion'],img='Shango',dateNote='Legendary reign; traditionally placed around the 14th–15th century'),
X('osun-osogbo','The Osun-Osogbo grove: a sacred forest saved by a river goddess and an Austrian artist',H,'confirmed',3,1950,['oral tradition','archaeological'],
 "The Osun-Osogbo Sacred Grove, on the banks of the Osun river outside Osogbo in south-west Nigeria, is one of the last of the sacred forests that once surrounded Yoruba towns. It is dedicated to Ọ̀ṣun, the orisha of fresh water, fertility and love. Tradition says that the town's founders were told by the river goddess to settle on higher ground, promising protection in return for worship, and an annual Osun-Osogbo festival renews this pact and draws large crowds each August. In the mid-20th century the grove was neglected and partly logged. The Austrian artist Susanne Wenger (1915–2009), who became a Yoruba priestess known as Adunni Olorisha, worked with local artists of the New Sacred Art movement to restore the shrines and fill the forest with large sculptures. The grove became a national monument in 1965 and a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2005.",
 [('Restoration or reinvention?','Some critics argue that Wenger\'s modernist sculptures changed the grove\'s character; many local worshippers and UNESCO see her work as saving the site and helping the tradition survive.'),
  ('Sacred groves worldwide','Sacred forests protected by religious taboos, from Yoruba groves to the sacred groves of India and ancient Europe, are seen by ecologists as an old form of nature conservation.')],
 ['wiki:Osun-Osogbo','wiki:Susanne_Wenger'],img='Osun-Osogbo',dateNote='Grove centuries old; restoration from the 1950s; UNESCO 2005'),
X('thunderstones','Thunderstones: prehistoric axes believed to be thunderbolts fallen from the sky',H,'debunked',2,1565,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Across much of the world, people who found polished prehistoric stone axe heads or flint arrowheads in the soil believed they had fallen from the sky with lightning. In Greek and Latin they were ceraunia, 'thunderbolts'; Romans placed them in buildings for protection; in medieval Europe they were prized as amulets against lightning, illness and witchcraft, and in Scandinavia they were offered beer and butter. Similar beliefs existed in Asia, the Americas and Africa, where Yoruba worshippers keep them at shrines of the thunder god Shango. Fossils such as sea urchins and belemnites were also called thunderstones. In the late 16th century the Vatican naturalist Michele Mercati argued that ceraunia were tools made by early people who did not use metal, and in 1723 Antoine de Jussieu compared them with stone tools from the Americas. By the 19th century they had become key evidence for the 'Stone Age' and the deep antiquity of humans.",
 [('A universal human reasoning','Folklorists note that the same idea arose independently in many places because stone tools turn up after rain has washed away soil, where lightning seems to have struck.'),
  ('Still used','In many places thunderstones remain protective objects, a living belief that coexists with the archaeological explanation.')],
 ['wiki:Thunderstone_(folklore)','wiki:Stone_tool'],img='Thunderstone (folklore)',dateNote='Belief ancient; Mercati\'s work c. 1570s–1590s (published 1717)'),
]
# --- part 3: why we die, the hare in the moon, and the first farmers, potters and dog-keepers
E+=[
X('origin-of-death-myths','Why we die: the lost message, the shed skin and the stone and the banana',M,'myth-legend',2,-3000,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "Nearly every culture has a story of how death entered a world where people once lived for ever, and folklorists find that the stories fall into a few recurring types spread across whole regions. In much of Africa, death comes through a message that went wrong: in a Khoikhoi story recorded by Wilhelm Bleek (1864), the Moon sent the hare to tell people that, just as the Moon dies and rises again, so would they, but the hare said they would die and perish, and the angry Moon split its lip; in the Zulu version a slow chameleon is overtaken by a lizard. In Oceania and Southeast Asia people once shed their skins like snakes and were renewed, until a grandchild failed to recognise a rejuvenated grandmother and she put her old skin back on. In the 'stone and banana' story told among the Poso of Sulawesi, the creator lowered a stone from the sky, people refused it and asked for a banana instead, and so their lives became like the banana, which dies after bearing fruit, rather than like enduring stone. Māori tradition tells how Māui tried to win immortality by crawling through the body of Hine-nui-te-pō, goddess of death, and was crushed when a bird laughed.",
 [('Ancient shared roots?','The Russian folklorist Yuri Berezkin has argued that some motifs, such as the garbled message and the shed skin, are so widespread that they may go back to very early human migrations out of Africa. Others think they arose independently many times. Both ideas are hard to test.'),
  ('Religious accounts','In Genesis, death follows Adam and Eve\'s disobedience in Eden; in Greek myth it comes with Pandora. Believers read these as revealed truths or moral teachings, while comparative mythologists group them with other \'lost immortality\' stories.')],
 ['wiki:Origin_of_death','doi:10.2307/534498'],img='File:Chief Whatanui in front of Hinenuitepo meeting house at Te Whaiti, 1930 ATLIB 298789.png',dateNote='Oral traditions of unknown age; recorded mostly 19th–20th centuries'),
X('hainuwele','Hainuwele: the murdered coconut girl whose body became the first crops',M,'myth-legend',2,1937,['oral tradition'],
 "On the island of Seram in eastern Indonesia, the Wemale people tell how a man named Ameta found the first coconut and planted it; from a drop of his blood on its blossom grew a girl, Hainuwele ('coconut branch'). She could excrete valuable things such as porcelain dishes, gongs and gold earrings, which she handed out at a great nine-night dance. The jealous dancers buried her alive in a pit in the dance ground. Ameta dug her up, cut her body into pieces and buried them, and from them grew the tubers and other plants that people have eaten ever since. The goddess Satene then made people pass through a spiral gate: those who passed remained human but became mortal, the others became animals and spirits. The myth was recorded by the German ethnologist Adolf Jensen after a 1937–38 expedition, and he used it to define the 'dema deity', a primordial being whose killing brings food plants and death into the world.",
 [('A memory of the first farming?','Jensen argued that such myths belong to early tuber-growing cultures and express an ancient idea that life feeds on death. Critics, including Jonathan Z. Smith, argued the myth also reflects colonial-era experiences, such as the arrival of trade goods.'),
  ('Parallels worldwide','Similar stories of crops springing from a slain body occur in the Americas (the maize of a slain mother in some Native American traditions), Japan (the food goddess Ōgetsu-hime) and elsewhere; mythologists debate whether they share an origin.')],
 ['wiki:Hainuwele','wiki:Ōgetsu-hime'],img='Hainuwele',dateNote='Recorded 1937–38; oral tradition of unknown age'),
X('moon-rabbit','The rabbit in the Moon: one shape seen in Asia and the Americas',M,'myth-legend',2,-300,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "Where some cultures see a man's face in the dark markings of the full Moon, much of Asia and Mesoamerica sees a rabbit or hare. In the Buddhist Śaśa Jātaka, a hare who had nothing else to offer a hungry holy man threw itself into a fire to give him its own body; the man was the god Śakra in disguise, who drew the hare's image on the Moon in its honour. In China the Jade Rabbit (Yutu) pounds the elixir of immortality for the Moon goddess Chang'e, and in Japan and Korea the rabbit pounds rice cakes. In the Aztec creation story recorded by Bernardino de Sahagún, the gods threw a rabbit into the face of the Moon to dim it so it would not shine as brightly as the Sun. China's lunar rovers launched in 2013 and 2019 were named Yutu and Yutu-2.",
 [('Independent invention or contact?','Most scholars see the shared rabbit as independent pattern-seeing (pareidolia): the shape is genuinely rabbit-like when the Moon is viewed from mid-northern latitudes. A few writers have suggested ancient trans-Pacific contact; there is no evidence for this.'),
  ('Hare, moon and death','In Africa the hare is linked to the Moon in the Khoikhoi story of how death began, and the hare is a lunar animal in parts of Europe too, suggesting a very widespread association whose origin is unknown.')],
 ['wiki:Moon_rabbit','wiki:Chang\'e'],img='Moon rabbit',dateNote='Jātaka tales compiled from c. 4th century BCE; Aztec account recorded 16th century'),
X('ohalo-ii','Ohalo II: a 23,000-year-old camp where people first tried growing cereals',H,'confirmed',2,-21000,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "In 1989, a drought lowered the Sea of Galilee in Israel and exposed the remains of a hunter-gatherer camp, Ohalo II, excavated by Dani Nadel. Dated to about 23,000 years ago, at the height of the last Ice Age, it was preserved under water and silt, so that plant remains survived in extraordinary quantity. The camp had six brushwood huts, the oldest known, a grass bedding floor, hearths and the grave of a man. Researchers recovered tens of thousands of seeds and fruits from more than 140 species, including wild wheat, barley, oats and figs, plus a grinding stone with traces of starch, flint sickle blades with use-wear from cutting cereals, and the seeds of weeds that typically grow in cultivated fields. A 2015 study argued this shows small-scale trial cultivation some 11,000 years before farming proper began.",
 [('Cultivation or wild harvest?','Snir and colleagues (2015) read the \'proto-weeds\' as signs of early cultivation; others argue the weeds could have grown among wild cereal stands, so the camp shows intensive wild harvesting rather than farming.'),
  ('A long road to farming','The site is part of a growing body of evidence, with sites like Shubayqa 1 in Jordan, where charred bread about 14,400 years old was found, that people experimented with cereals long before the Neolithic, challenging the idea of a sudden agricultural \'revolution\'.')],
 ['wiki:Ohalo_II','doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0131422','doi:10.1073/pnas.1801071115'],img='File:Composite Sickles for Cereal Harvesting at 23,000-Years-Old Ohalo II, Israel.jpg',dateNote='c. 23,000 years before present'),
X('abu-hureyra','Abu Hureyra: the village that saw the first farming, now under a lake, and a disputed cosmic impact',H,'disputed',2,-11300,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "Tell Abu Hureyra in the Euphrates valley of northern Syria was excavated in 1972–73 by Andrew Moore in a rescue project before the Tabqa Dam flooded it under Lake Assad. Its deposits span about 13,300 to 7,800 years ago, from a village of settled hunter-gatherers (Abu Hureyra 1) to one of the world's early farming towns (Abu Hureyra 2). It was one of the first sites where flotation was used to recover tiny plant remains. The archaeobotanist Gordon Hillman argued that people there began cultivating rye at the start of the cold, dry Younger Dryas period around 13,000 years ago, possibly the earliest cereal cultivation known, as wild foods became scarce. Since 2012 Moore and colleagues have reported meltglass, nanodiamonds, microspherules and platinum-group metals in the layer at the end of the first village, which they attribute to a cosmic airburst at the onset of the Younger Dryas.",
 [('Earliest cultivators?','Hillman\'s claim of Younger Dryas rye cultivation is debated: some archaeobotanists argue the grains are too few, or intrusive from later layers, to prove cultivation.'),
  ('Younger Dryas impact','The 2020 paper by Moore et al. in Scientific Reports argued for an impact at Abu Hureyra; it received a formal correction in 2025. Many archaeologists and impact scientists, for example in a 2023 review led by Vance Holliday, reject the Younger Dryas impact hypothesis; supporters maintain the evidence at Abu Hureyra and other sites is strong.')],
 ['wiki:Tell_Abu_Hureyra','doi:10.1038/s41598-020-60867-w','doi:10.1016/j.earscirev.2023.104502'],img='File:Abu Hureyra grinding stones Ashmolean Museum.jpg',end=-5800),
X('guila-naquitz','Guilá Naquitz and the riddle of maize: how a scraggly grass became the grain of the Americas',H,'confirmed',2,-8000,['archaeological','radiocarbon','genetic'],
 "Guilá Naquitz, a small cave in the Oaxaca valley of Mexico excavated by Kent Flannery in 1966, held some of the earliest evidence of farming in the Americas: seeds of domesticated squash (Cucurbita pepo) dated to about 10,000 years ago, and the oldest known maize cobs, tiny ears about 6,250 years old. Maize looks very different from any wild plant, and for decades its origin was a puzzle. Genetic studies, notably Matsuoka and colleagues in 2002, showed it was domesticated once, about 9,000 years ago, from a wild grass called teosinte in the Balsas river valley of south-western Mexico, where maize starch about 8,700 years old has since been found on grinding stones. Teosinte has a few hard-cased kernels; over thousands of years of selection farmers turned it into the large, many-rowed cob that now feeds much of the world.",
 [('An extinct wild maize','Before the genetic evidence, the botanist Paul Mangelsdorf argued that maize came from a now-extinct wild maize crossed with the grass Tripsacum. This view has been abandoned by most scientists, though it held sway for decades.'),
  ('Gift of the gods','In Mesoamerican traditions maize is sacred: the Popol Vuh says the gods made humans from maize dough, and many peoples tell of maize being found inside a mountain or given by a deity, stories that parallel origin myths of crops elsewhere.')],
 ['wiki:Guilá_Naquitz_Cave','doi:10.1073/pnas.98.4.2101','doi:10.1073/pnas.052125199'],img='File:Teosinte and Modern Corn Comparison (3745571067).jpg',dateNote='Squash c. 10,000 years ago; maize cobs c. 6,250 years ago'),
X('dog-domestication','The first dogs: when, where and how wolves joined humans',H,'disputed',2,-13000,['archaeological','genetic','radiocarbon'],
 "The dog was the first domesticated animal, tamed by hunter-gatherers long before farming. The oldest generally accepted dog is the Bonn–Oberkassel dog from Germany, about 14,000 years old, buried with a man and a woman; it had survived a serious illness as a puppy, suggesting it was cared for. Genetic studies show that dogs descend from a population of Ice Age wolves, now extinct, which split from the ancestors of modern wolves between about 40,000 and 20,000 years ago. Where and when domestication took place is still debated: proposals include Europe, Siberia, East Asia and the Near East, or more than one place. A 2022 study of ancient wolf genomes found that dogs carry ancestry from at least two wolf populations, an eastern and a western one.",
 [('Very early dogs?','Some researchers, such as Mietje Germonpré, have identified skulls up to about 36,000 years old (for example from Goyet Cave in Belgium) as early dogs; others think they are unusual wolves.'),
  ('Who tamed whom?','One view is that people captured wolf pups; another is that wolves domesticated themselves by scavenging around human camps, with the tamest thriving. Some suggest Ice Age hunters had surplus meat to share with wolves in winter.')],
 ['wiki:Domestication_of_the_dog','wiki:Bonn–Oberkassel_dog','doi:10.1038/s41586-022-04824-9'],img='Bonn–Oberkassel dog',dateNote='Oldest accepted dog c. 14,000 years ago'),
X('xianren-cave','Xianren Cave: the world\'s oldest pottery, made by Ice Age hunters',H,'confirmed',2,-18000,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "In Xianren (Xianrendong) Cave in Jiangxi province, southern China, archaeologists found fragments of pottery dated by radiocarbon to about 20,000–19,000 years ago, published in Science in 2012 as the oldest known pottery in the world. Many sherds have scorch marks, suggesting they were used for cooking, perhaps for extracting fat and marrow or for making alcoholic drinks. The pots were made by mobile hunter-gatherers during the last Ice Age, about 10,000 years before farming began in the region. Nearby layers of the cave and the Diaotonghuan rock shelter also show the use of wild rice and later early rice cultivation. The find overturned the old idea that pottery was invented by settled farmers.",
 [('Why make pots in the Ice Age?','Some researchers argue that cooking in pots helped people survive the cold, food-scarce Last Glacial Maximum by releasing more nutrition from food; others think the pots were used for feasting or brewing at special times.'),
  ('East Asian pottery first','Early pottery also appears in Japan (Jōmon) and the Russian Far East around 16,000 years ago, supporting the view that pottery began among East Asian foragers.')],
 ['wiki:Xianren_Cave','doi:10.1126/science.1218643'],img='Xianren Cave',dateNote='c. 20,000–19,000 years before present'),
]
